   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794744",
   "speaker": "Kim Carr",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-18",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794745",
   "speaker": "Kim Carr",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-18",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The government announced the Renewable Energy (Electricity) Amendment Bill 2009 to expand the renewable energy target to 45,000 gigawatt-hours by 2020, aiming for 20% of electricity from renewable sources, with a shortfall charge increased to $65 per megawatt-hour and a Solar Credits mechanism for small-scale systems. The coalition supported the target but insisted on amendments to provide exemptions for emissions-intensive trade-exposed industries like aluminium and food processing, while the Greens pushed for a 30% target and opposed including coal seam methane and native forest burning."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794832",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-19",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794833",
   "speaker": "Ian Macdonald",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-19",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The coalition supports a 20% renewable energy target by 2020 and the legislation to establish it, subject to government negotiations on amendments including full decoupling from the emissions trading scheme, assistance for the aluminium and food processing sectors, and reserving a portion of targets for emerging technologies like baseload solar and geothermal. The Mackay Sugar cogeneration project in Queensland, a $100 million initiative generating 206,000 megawatts annually and saving 200,000 tonnes of greenhouse gases, is cited as dependent on the bill's passage to secure renewable energy certificate pricing."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794834",
   "speaker": "Penny Wong",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-19",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Senator Milne moved Australian Greens amendments to remove solar hot water heaters, heat pumps and native forest biomass from the renewable energy target, arguing they crowd out genuine renewable generation and citing that solar and heat-pump systems produced 24 per cent of renewable energy certificates to the end of 2008. She also questioned the cost impact on Catholic Health, estimated at $10.8 million from the RET and CPRS combined, and opposed eligibility for energy from native forest burning, including the proposed Gunns 184 MW furnace."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794874",
   "speaker": "Gavin Marshall",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-19",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The speaker argued that the proposed Gunns pulp mill furnace in Tasmania, which would burn native forests, should not be eligible for renewable energy certificates under the Renewable Energy (Electricity) (Charge) Amendment Bill 2009. The speaker also opposed the deal between the government and the coalition, stating it would subsidise the destruction of native forests and squeeze out genuine renewable energy."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-794896",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2009-08-20",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
